<blockquote>
<p>You nearly made me feel bad for a literal Nazi, which is impressive.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>*Ben Grimm voice on* It's NITPICKING TIME! *Ben Grimm voice off*</p>
<p>There are various interpretations of what's considered a &quot;Nazi&quot; when you refer to German military personnel during WWII. There are some people who use the word &quot;Nazi&quot; interchangeably with &quot;German&quot; for the period, but that's IMHO way too broad a view; many in the Wehrmacht did not share the views of the leadership, and were fighting because they were conscripted or out of a general desire to protect their homeland.</p>
<p>When it comes to military command positions, it gets even thornier. Many of the German High Command were there prior to the Third Reich, and in fact had a great distaste for the politics of the era, but served out of perceived duty. Other were enthusiastic and commanded SS divisions and such. I'm not sure what the particulatrs of our skip's situation are, but I don't know if he's automatically a &quot;Nazi&quot; as such.</p>

							</div>

								
					
